.TH "ELEMENT.AUXCLICK_EVENT" "3JS" "August 15, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.auxclick_event \- Element: auxclick event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]auxclick\f[B]\f[R] event is fired at an
\f[CR]Element\f[R] when a non\-primary pointing device button (any mouse
button other than the primary\(emusually leftmost\(embutton) has been
pressed and released both within the same element.
.PP
\f[CR]auxclick\f[R] is fired after the \f[CR]mousedown\f[R] and
\f[CR]mouseup\f[R] events have been fired, in that order.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in methods like \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R], or set
an event handler property.
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqauxclick\(dq, (event) => { })

onauxclick = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A \f[CR]PointerEvent\f[R].
Inherits from \f[CR]MouseEvent\f[R].
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] In earlier versions of the specification, the event type
for this event was a \f[CR]MouseEvent\f[R].
Check browser compatibility for more information.
.RE
.SH PREVENTING DEFAULT ACTIONS
For the vast majority of browsers that map middle click to opening a
link in a new tab, including Firefox, it is possible to cancel this
behavior by calling \f[CR]preventDefault()\f[R] from within an
\f[CR]auxclick\f[R] event handler.
.PP
When listening for \f[CR]auxclick\f[R] events originating on elements
that do not support input or navigation, you will often want to
explicitly prevent other default actions mapped to the down action of
the middle mouse button.
On Windows this is usually autoscroll, and on Linux this is usually
clipboard paste.
This can be done by preventing the default behavior of the
\f[CR]mousedown\f[R] or \f[CR]pointerdown\f[R] event.
.PP
Additionally, you may need to avoid opening a system context menu after
a right click.
Due to timing differences between operating systems, this too is not a
preventable default behavior of \f[CR]auxclick\f[R].
Instead, this can be done by preventing the default behavior of the
\f[CR]contextmenu\f[R] event.
.SH EXAMPLES
In this example we define functions for two event handlers \(em
\f[CR]onclick\f[R] and \f[CR]onauxclick\f[R].
The former changes the color of the button background, while the latter
changes the button foreground (text) color.
You also can see the two functions in action by trying the demo out with
a multi\-button mouse (\c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/dom-examples/auxclick/
see it live on GitHub
.UE \c
; also \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/blob/main/auxclick/index.html
see the source code
.UE \c
).
.SS JavaScript
.IP
.EX
\f[B]let\f[R] button = document.querySelector(\(dqbutton\(dq);
\f[B]let\f[R] html = document.querySelector(\(dqhtml\(dq);

\f[B]function\f[R] random(number) {
  \f[B]return\f[R] Math.floor(Math.random() * number);
}

\f[B]function\f[R] randomColor() {
  \f[B]return\f[R] \(gargb(${random(255)} ${random(255)} ${random(255)})\(ga;
}

button.onclick = () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  button.style.backgroundColor = randomColor();
};

button.onauxclick = (e) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  e.preventDefault();
  button.style.color = randomColor();
};

button.oncontextmenu = (e) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  e.preventDefault();
};
.EE
.PP
Notice that in addition to capturing the \f[CR]auxclick\f[R] event using
\f[CR]onauxclick\f[R], the \f[CR]contextmenu\f[R] event is also
captured, and \f[CR]preventDefault()\f[R] called on that event, in order
to prevent the context menu from popping up after the color change is
applied.
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]button\f[R]>Click me!</\f[B]button\f[R]>
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] If you are using a three\-button mouse, you\(cqll notice
that the \f[CR]onauxclick\f[R] handler is run when any of the non\-left
mouse buttons are clicked (usually including any \(lqspecial\(rq buttons
on gaming mice).
.RE
